in dagenham the fire is out  one resident who used to call this  cul-de-sac home was escorted by the fire  service through her back garden  to see for the first time just how much  she has lost  i've lived in this house for 32 years  loads of memories  everything completely gone  a whole lot  animals missing  so i'm here today to try and find my  cats  i'm just absolutely lost for those  modest  i'm sorry  even though the worst of the heaters  passed there were more fires today this  one in upminster brought quickly under  control by exhausted firefighters many  of whom have never experienced times  like this  london fire brigade had its busiest  period yesterday since world war ii  uh it resulted in  us receiving 2 600 fight calls  and attending over 1 100 incidents  and significantly different too  moorland and grass fires are common in  the uk but seeing them go on to destroy  homes in multiple locations on the same  day is most definitely not  we associate scenes like this with  hotter drier countries than our own but  climate scientists have been warning us  for decades that as our planet warms  pushing up average temperatures the  chance of heat waves will only increase  and with them the risk of fire so while  scenes like this of wildfires destroying  people's properties are a new reality  for the uk  they should come as no surprise to us at  all  if you get hotter temperatures and lower  humidity and higher winds those are all  conditions that make fires more  prevalent if the ignition source is  there countries where fire is a real  problem like australia they know more  about how to manage urban areas and try  to minimize the amount of flammable  vegetation for example around their  homes according to the latest national  risk assessment the annual number of  wildfires in the uk has increased  significantly since 2016.  the number of days when fire risk is  high is predicted to rise even further  doubling if we warm by two degrees  celsius and quadrupling 